3842a0e53fcacf439250245935fe7a7ad068973ee5f896f11916c3881e57.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x+xz,\\,y,\\,z-{x}^{2}⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $\\sqrt{5}$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356457 1113 #uuid "458ea386-13da-5ecb-816f-36e185865cd0" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356457 1145 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=\\sqrt{5}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=5$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x+xz,\\,y,\\,z-{x}^{2}⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}⟨x+xz,\\,y,\\,z-{x}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(x(x+xz)+{y}^{2}+z(z-{x}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}({x}^{2}+{x}^{2}z+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}-{x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot (\\sqrt{5})^{2} \\\\  & =20\\pi \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356458 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356459 13194139535588 true] [17592186356458 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356460 13194139535588 true] [17592186356458 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356461 13194139535588 true] [17592186356458 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356462 13194139535588 true] [17592186356