 #uuid "5b03b044-5d71-5b5b-8c51-953fb03cf59a" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356464 1145 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-{x}^{2}z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-{x}^{2}z⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}+0-{x}^{2}{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356465 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356466 13194139535588 true] [17592186356465 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356467 13194139535588 true] [17592186356465 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356468 13194139535588 true] [17592186356465 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356469 13194139535588 true] [17592186356465 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356470 13194139535588 true] [17592186356465 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535588 true] [17592186356465 1041 "selection" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356465 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356468 13194139535588 true] [17592186356465 1110 #uuid "b060f4a0-a27d-5af7-a8a0-b3b893c69ac8" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356466 1034 "12\\pi " 13194139535588 true] [17592186356466 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535588 true] [17592186356466 1111 #uuid "4e1b360e-5964-50af-bd7d-954945ba7b8b" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356467 1034 "3\\pi " 13194139535588 true] [17592186356467 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535588 true] [17592186356467 1111 #uuid "5edffc1c-606f-569d-9d2b-c605f4049869" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356468 1034 "0" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356468 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535588 true] [17592186356468 1111 #uuid "97047edb-fa2d-5d24-87b6-c66922eaed6c" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356469 1034 "4\\pi \\sqrt{3}" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356469 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535588 true] [17592186356469 1111 #uuid "d3c44cc4-e567-57b2-8bec-517997c14c3d" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356470 1034 "18\\pi " 13194139535588 true] [17592186356470 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535588 true] [17592186356470 1111 #uuid "a2019e5f-1288-5a16-a602-1e77dd34c37c" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356471 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356472 13194139535588 true] [17592186356471 1089 "q-162560" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356471 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535588 true] [17592186356471 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/04f58e972500033523e3ca3247ea4e1c5c377d8dc2b402b1c0200d9c85529894.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356471 1113 #uuid "3e6626d5-eaee-5da4-ade0-3b113a418809" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356471 1145 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the right and left sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/8652a197f603c517cd462c69473e3a693ba8dcd76340b6241f890663356e00cb.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,6{x}^{2}+2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}6{x}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6{x}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}6{x}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2{x}^{3}+2xz]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}16+4z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[16z+2{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-2z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-2{∬}_{D}z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-2\\cdot [x]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =18+(-2) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356472 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356473 13194139535588 true] [17592186356472 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356474 13194139535588 true] [17592186356472 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356475 13194139535588 true] [17592186356472 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356476 13194139535588 true] [17592186356472 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356477 13194139535588 true] [17592186356472 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535588 true] [17592186356472 1041 "selection" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356472 1104 #edb/ref 17592186356475 13194139535588 true] [17592186356472 1110 #uuid "09e878cb-2673-5a04-b7eb-be87c08da50b" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356473 1034 "12" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356473 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535588 true] [17592186356473 1111 #uuid "b4314c74-82e8-58d1-ae65-02b96e9bc19e" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356474 1034 "20" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356474 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535588 true] [17592186356474 1111 #uuid "d8564fc0-1d94-519c-8eed-e33c7e25300e" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356475 1034 "16" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356475 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535588 true] [17592186356475 1111 #uuid "d6b9de8c-d171-5b0b-a6f6-5e565ebda64b" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356476 1034 "18" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356476 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535588 true] [17592186356476 1111 #uuid "b2ba1a5e-f930-5436-968e-ec83791f2625" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356477 1034 "24" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356477 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535588 true] [17592186356477 1111 #uuid "2651a9d3-d6e5-59ad-924a-377b0c7a819f" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356478 1017 #edb/ref 17592186356479 13194139535588 true] [17592186356478 1089 "q-162563" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356478 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535588 true] [17592186356478 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/638a02dec15c2cecd8563854c2fb071ff70f10004f60339ef92d0914e7198ebc.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356478 1113 #uuid "82cb2475-3100-5a72-830f-ebf6c4d02e82" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356478 1145 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $x$-component of $F=⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $x$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the front and back sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14068126/assets/4fd228a9bf0a2d8382139d3f804ee7c9b3fd8721c8ad079de43e61b9e8947240.png)\n\n  \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the front surface. On this surface, we have $x=1$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=1$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =4+3{y}^{2}+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4+3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4+3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4+3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[(4+2z)y+{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}4+2z+1\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2z+5\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[{z}^{2}+5z]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =9+15 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the back surface. On this surface, we have $x=0$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-(3{y}^{2}+2z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-(3{y}^{2}+2z)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[{y}^{3}+2yz]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}1+2z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{[z+{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-(3+9) \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =24+(-12) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535588 true] [17592186356479 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356480 13194139535588 true] [17592186356479 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356481 13194139535588 true] [17592186356479 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356482 13194139535588 true] [17592186356479 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356483 13194139535588 true] [17592186356479 1000 #edb/ref 17592186356484 13194139535588 true] [17592186356479 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 131941395355